{"name":"Parachute Silk","hex":"#ffe2b5","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"parachute","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A device, generally constructed from fabric, that is designed to employ air resistance to control the fall of an object or person, causing them to float instead of falling.","A web or fold of skin extending between the legs of gliding mammals, such as the flying squirrel and colugo."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To jump, fall, descend, etc. using such a device.","To introduce into a place using such a device."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/parachute"},{"term":"silk","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A fine fiber excreted by the silkworm or other arthropod (such as a spider).","A thread or yarn made from silk fibers."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To develop silk.","To remove the silk from (corn)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/silk"}],"entries":[{"title":"Artificial silk","blocks":[{"text":"Artificial silk or art silk is any synthetic fiber which resembles silk, but typically costs less to produce. Frequently, the term artificial silk is just a synonym for rayon."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/34/How_a_British_Woman_Dresses_in_Wartime-_Utility_Clothing_in_Britain%2C_1943_D14784.jpg/330px-How_a_British_Woman_Dresses_in_Wartime-_Utility_Clothing_in_Britain%2C_1943_D14784.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_silk","wikidata":"Q15124338"},{"title":"Parachute","blocks":[{"text":"A parachute is a device designed to slow an object's descent through an atmosphere by creating drag or aerodynamic lift. It is primarily used to safely support people exiting aircraft at height, but also serves various purposes like slowing cargo, aiding in space capsule recovery on Earth, landing spacecraft on other planets, and stabilizing vehicles or objects. Modern parachutes are typically made from durable fabrics like nylon and come in various shapes, such as dome-shaped, rectangular, and inverted domes, depending on their specific function."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9a/Bangladesh_Air_Force_%28BAF%29_paratroopers_jump_from_a_U.S._Air_Force_C-130_Hercules_aircraft_over_Bangladesh_during_exercise_Cope_South_14_Nov._10%2C_2013_131110-F-SI013-240.jpg/330px-thumbnail.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Parachute","wikidata":"Q482816"},{"title":"Silk","blocks":[{"text":"Silk is a natural protein fiber, some forms of which can be woven or knitted into textiles. The protein fiber of silk is composed mainly of fibroin (75-80%) and a smaller coating of sericin (20-25%). It is most commonly produced from the cocoons of certain insect larvae. The best-known silk is obtained from the cocoons of the larvae of the mulberry silkworm Bombyx mori, which are reared in captivity (sericulture)."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2e/Silk_raw_01a.jpg/330px-Silk_raw_01a.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Silk","wikidata":"Q37681"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b1/Conical_Parachute%2C_1470s%2C_British_Museum_Add._MSS_34%2C113%2C_fol._200v.jpg/500px-Conical_Parachute%2C_1470s%2C_British_Museum_Add._MSS_34%2C113%2C_fol._200v.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b1/Conical_Parachute%2C_1470s%2C_British_Museum_Add._MSS_34%2C113%2C_fol._200v.jpg/500px-Conical_Parachute%2C_1470s%2C_British_Museum_Add._MSS_34%2C113%2C_fol._200v.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The oldest known depiction of a parachute, attributed to Francesco di Giorgio Martini (I